Q: How to add the return type of a custom query in RTK query? A: <p>You generally define the return type at the endpoints, see <a href="https://redux-toolkit.js.org/rtk-query/usage-with-typescript#typing-query-and-mutation-endpoints" rel="nofollow noreferrer">Typing query and mutation endpoints</a> for details. <em>You can</em> <a href="https://redux-toolkit.js.org/rtk-query/usage-with-typescript#typing-a-basequery" rel="nofollow noreferrer">type</a> the <code>baseQuery</code> return type, but this is only if/when you expect <em><strong>all</strong></em> queries and mutations to return the same type. Because of this it's recommended to leave the base query return type as <code>unknown</code> as you have in your code.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>How to add the return type of a custom query in RTK query?</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Each query and mutation endpoint can be typed to the types provided to the generics in <code>&lt;ResultType, QueryArg&gt;</code>.</p>
<p>Examples:</p>
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>endpoints: build =&gt; ({
  // ...
  getUserById: build.query&lt;UserType, string&gt;({
    query: (id) =&gt; `users/${id}`,
  }),
  updateUser: build.mutation&lt;UserType, UserType&gt;({
    query: (user) =&gt; ({
      url: `users/${user.id}`,
      method: &quot;post&quot;,
      body: user
    })
  }),
  // ...
}),
</code></pre>
<p>Since you don't appear to have a dedicated endpoint definition for the token refresh you'll basically just do exactly what you did when dispatching the <code>setNewAccessToken</code> action where you cast the <code>unknown</code>-typed query result to <code>RefreshTokenResponse</code> for the action payload. Anywhere you reference this result value you will cast it to <code>RefreshTokenResponse</code> before using it.</p>

Q: Does the Java Memory Model permit reordering writes to a volatile variable into a loop? A: <p>The following explanation is taken from <a href="https://ricardogeek.com/concurrencia-en-java-volatile/" rel="nofollow noreferrer">this page</a>, since it's in Spanish, I've translated it.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Definition:</p>
<p><code>volatile</code> is a lightweight form of synchronization that addresses visibility and ordering issues, is used as a field modifier.<br />
The purpose of <code>volatile</code> is to ensure that when a thread writes a value to a field, the written value is “immediately available” to any thread that reads it subsequently.</p>
<p>When to Use <code>volatile</code></p>
<p>We can use <code>volatile</code> variables only when all of the following criteria are met:</p>
<ul>
<li><p>Writes to the variable do not depend on its current value, or you can ensure that only one thread updates the value;</p>
</li>
<li><p>The variable does not participate in invariants with other state variables;</p>
</li>
<li><p>Locking is not required for any other reason while the variable is being accessed.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<p>Some appropriate scenarios are:</p>
<ul>
<li>When we have a simple indicator (such as a completion, interruption, or status indicator) or another primitive data element that is accessed (read) by multiple threads;</li>
<li>When we have a more complex immutable object that is initialized by one thread and then accessed by others: here we want to update the reference to the immutable data structure and allow other threads to reference it.</li>
</ul>
</blockquote>
<p>One logical conclusion that can be drawn from the text above is that it makes no sense to use a <code>volatile</code> keyword within synchronized code.</p>
<p>On the other hand, the issues mentioned in the post you linked to date back to before the year 2000, so I’m not sure if they’re still relevant today. Plus, they refer to variables referenced within the body of the class that implements the thread, which isn’t the case here.</p>
<pre><code>  // using a *volatile* variable within a
  // synchronized context makes no sense
long foo;

protected long getFoo() {
   if( foo == null ) {
      synchronized( this ) {     
         if( foo == null ) { 
            do {

                 // we don't need to use another variable
               foo = generateFoo();
            }
            while( lookupFoo( foo ) != null );
         }
      }    
   }
   return foo;
}
</code></pre>
